### Strengths — evidence with file:line

- The commitment is not merely another audit-local hash: it is part of the
  trusted hash-chained reconciliation record, and both the live publication
  path and public verification reconstruct its full closed fact set
  (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1500`,
  `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1572`,
  `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:3040`).
- Recovery ordering is unambiguous: recover untrusted facts, authenticate the
  committed package, restore BudgetLedger, then re-verify the already durable
  reconciliation (`agent_loop.py:3961`, `agent_loop.py:3967`,
  `agent_loop.py:3974`, `agent_loop.py:3979`).
- Idempotency covers the full terminal package and terminal lease closure, not
  just provider facts (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:3056`,
  `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:3075`,
  `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:3100`).
- The start-commitment regression exercises the exact post-publication failure
  boundary and observes the externally relevant invariants, including zero
  sends and fresh-process replay (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:4966`,
  `tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5026`,
  `tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5060`).
- New durable fields are closed identities, digests, lifecycle, usage, charge,
  and terminal facts; they do not add prompt, candidate source, raw response,
  credential, header, key, or approval content
  (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1500`).

